      "location": "sections/homily/10-body.tex:48\u201351",
      "problem": "Immediately after naming the Collect, \u2018the fruit of our work\u2019 blurs whose work the prayer names. At Missal p. 423, no. 1770, the faithful pursue the fruit of God's work and ask to receive greater remedies. The passage already preserves the faithful's dependence on God, so this is a local imprecision.",
      "required_result": "Preserve the difference between God's work and the faithful's response in the sentence explaining the Collect. For example, \u2018We ask God to stir our wills so that we will follow through on what he has begun in us and receive the healing we still need.\u2019",
      "severity": "advisory"
    },
    {
      "id": "HOM-STUDY-FLIGHT-AUDIENCE-001",
      "location": "sections/30-deliverance-and-waiting.tex:114\u2013115 and sections/40-prayer-and-conversion.tex:273\u2013279",
      "problem": "\u2018When they will have to flee\u2019 and \u2018their flight\u2019 make the disciples who hear the reply sound like the particular people who will flee. Matthew 24:16 directs the flight to those in Judea, and Chrysostom's homily 76.1 explicitly distinguishes the apostles in his treatment of the Sabbath. The spoken homily separates the two groups.",
      "required_result": "In the two study passages, say that Jesus tells his hearers to pray about the flight from Judea and names those in Judea as the people who must flee. Retain both the promise for the elect and the argument about asking for help while danger persists.",
      "severity": "advisory"
    }
