  "summary": "I read homily.tex, its spoken body and its terminal note, and compared them with the two study interpretations. I checked the 1962 Missal images at printed pp. 422\u2013424 and rubric 18, the dated Ordo entry, Lasance/Walsh pp. 737 and 740, the registered Challoner passages, Chrysostom's Matthew homily 76, Aquinas's Colossians lecture at anchor 87848, Theophylact at fols. 69r\u2013v, and Bede's Latin transcription at line 339. Matthew's prayer about the flight and promise of shortened days support asking for relief while danger persists. Mark's command to forgive while praying and the Secret's petition for changed desires give that trust a concrete act before relief comes. The speech is intelligible on one hearing and reaches Eucharistic hope through the Postcommunion. TeXcount confirms 1,156 spoken words, or an estimated 10.1\u201310.5 minutes at 110\u2013115 words per minute. This is a source and speech PASS with two local advisories."
